  "resources": {
    "costumbres": {
      "c-31db93c979ecbf22": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-640",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "See also Luke 12:58. According to the Roman law, if a person had a quarrel which he could not settle privately he had the right to order his adversary to accompany him to the praetor. If he refused, the prosecutor took some one present to witness by saying, “May I take you to wit…"
      },
      "c-5366805916e4c587": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-641",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "The Pharisees taught that there were two kinds of oaths—the violation of one being perjury, and that of the other an innocent matter, or at most but a slight offense. If the name of God was in the oath it was binding; this the Saviour refers to in verse 33. If the name of God was…"
      },
      "c-8e8026187b0aae9b": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-642",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "There is reference here to an ancient Persian custom, which was adopted by the Persian government. The Persians introduced the use of regular couriers to carry letters or news. See note on Job 9:25 (#407). The king’s courier had absolute command of all help that was necessary in…"
      },
      "c-ab2479028216e443": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-639",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "See also Luke 16:17. There may be allusion here to the great care taken by the copyists of the law to secure accuracy even to the smallest letters, or curves or points of letters. ‘lima, “jot,” refers to the yodh,י , the smallest letter in the Hebrew alphabet; κεραία, “tittle,” i…"
      },
      "c-bdcb2806832a5e02": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-637",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "See also Luke 14:34-35. Salt produced by the evaporation of sea-water in hot countries is said sometimes to lose its saline properties. The same result is also sometimes seen in impure rock-salt that has long been exposed to the air. When such is the case there can nothing be don…"
      },
      "c-ed35cc9b09e2c937": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-638",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "See also Mark 4: 21; Luke 8:16; 11:33. The common lamps among the Greeks and Romans were made of clay, the more costly ones of bronze, and even sometimes of gold. Some of these were very beautiful. Most of the lamps were oval in shape and flat on top, on which there were often fi…"
      }
    },
